/* data/learn-c/phy-12-12.js - GENERATED by tools/build_learn.py from data/learn/summaries/phy-12-12.json (our own words;
 * the notes of one chapter, loaded when the chapter is opened). Do not edit by hand. */
(window.LEARN_C = window.LEARN_C || {})["phy-12-12"] = {"12.1":{"s":"Moving charges make magnetic fields. Faraday asked the reverse question: can a magnetic field make a current? Yes, if the field through a circuit changes. This is electromagnetic induction, the idea behind generators, transformers and induction cooktops."},"12.2":{"s":"Faraday's first law: whenever the magnetic flux linked with a circuit changes, an emf is induced in it. If the circuit is closed, a current flows. Second law: the size of the induced emf equals the rate of change of flux, e = −dΦ/dt.","f":[{"x":"e = -d(phi)/dt","n":"Faraday's law with Lenz's sign; phi = flux linked with the circuit.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 12.2, p.266"},{"x":"e = -N*d(phi)/dt","n":"Coil of N turns; phi = flux through one turn.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 12.2, p.266"}],"w":["Only a CHANGE in flux induces an emf; a large steady flux gives none."]},"12.3":{"s":"Lenz's law gives the direction: the induced current always opposes the change that caused it. Push a magnet's north pole into a coil and the near face of the coil becomes a north pole, pushing back. This is conservation of energy: you must do work to produce the current.","w":["The induced current opposes the change in flux, not the flux itself: with flux falling, it tries to keep it up.","If Lenz's law were the other way round, the current would grow by itself and break energy conservation."]},"12.4":{"s":"Magnetic flux through a surface is Φ = B·A = BA cos θ, the amount of field passing through it. Its unit is the weber. Flux can change if B changes, if the area changes, or if the angle between the field and the surface changes.","f":[{"x":"phi = B*A*cos(theta)","n":"Flux through a flat area A in a uniform field (phi = B·A); theta = angle between B and the normal to the area. In general phi = sum of B·da. Unit: weber (Wb).","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 12.4, p.268"}],"w":["θ is measured from the normal (area vector), not from the surface: flux is largest at θ = 0."]},"12.5":{"s":"A conductor of length l moving with speed v at right angles to a field B has an emf e = Blv across it. This is motional emf: the magnetic force pushes the charges along the rod. A rod rotating in a field also has an emf between its ends.","f":[{"x":"e = B*l*v","n":"Rod of length l moving at speed v at right angles to B.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 12.5, p.269"},{"x":"e = (1/2)*B*omega*l^2","n":"Rod of length l turning about one end at angular speed omega in a field along the axis of turning.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 12.5, p.270"}],"w":["A rod moving along its own length, or parallel to B, has no motional emf."]},"12.6":{"s":"A coil that does not move can still get an induced emf. It happens when the magnetic field through it changes. Examples: a magnet swinging past it, or a changing current in a nearby circuit. The emf is largest when the flux changes fastest."},"12.7":{"s":"An AC generator turns a coil in a magnetic field. The flux through it keeps changing, so an alternating emf e = e₀ sin ωt is produced, with e₀ = NBAω. Mechanical energy becomes electrical energy.","f":[{"x":"e = N*B*A*omega*sin(omega*t)","n":"AC generator: coil of N turns, area A, turning at angular speed omega.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 12.7, p.274"},{"x":"e_0 = N*B*A*omega","n":"Peak emf; reached when the coil's plane is parallel to B (flux zero, changing fastest).","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 12.7, p.274"}],"w":["The emf is largest when the flux through the coil is zero, and zero when the flux is largest."]},"12.8":{"s":"In a running motor, the turning coil also acts as a generator and produces an emf that opposes the supply: the back emf. It limits the current once the motor is up to speed. In a generator, the induced current produces a back torque that opposes the rotation, so work is needed to keep it turning.","w":["A motor draws a large current at start because there is no back emf yet; that is why starters limit the current."]},"12.9":{"s":"Pulling a loop out of a magnetic field induces a current, and the field pushes back on that current. The work you do equals the electrical energy produced, which ends up as heat in the loop's resistance. Induction never gives free energy.","f":[{"x":"i = B*L*v/R","n":"Current in a loop of resistance R pulled out of a field at speed v; L = side cutting the field.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 12.9, p.276"},{"x":"F = B^2*L^2*v/R","n":"Force needed to pull the loop at constant speed.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 12.9, p.276"},{"x":"P = B^2*L^2*v^2/R","n":"Mechanical power you supply; equals the heat produced, i^2 R.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 12.9, p.276"}]},"12.10":{"s":"A changing magnetic flux through a solid metal plate induces circling currents in it, called eddy currents. They cause heating and braking. Uses: magnetic braking, induction furnaces, damping of meters. Laminated cores in transformers reduce unwanted eddy currents.","w":["Slots or thin insulated sheets (lamination) cut the paths of eddy currents and so reduce the heating."]},"12.11":{"s":"When the current in a coil changes, its own flux changes and induces an emf that opposes the change: self-induction. e = −L di/dt, where L is the self-inductance in henry. For a long solenoid L = μ₀n²Al, so more turns and a larger area give more inductance.","f":[{"x":"N*phi = L*i","n":"Flux linkage of a coil carrying current i; L = self-inductance in henry.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 12.11, p.278"},{"x":"e = -L*di/dt","n":"Self-induced emf.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 12.11, p.278"},{"x":"L = mu_0*n^2*A*l","n":"Long solenoid; n = turns per unit length, A = cross-section, l = length.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 12.11, p.279"},{"x":"L = L_1 + L_2 + ...","n":"Inductors in series (no mutual coupling); in parallel 1/L = 1/L_1 + 1/L_2 + ...","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 12.11, p.280"}],"w":["Inductance depends only on the coil's shape, turns and core, not on the current."]},"12.12":{"s":"Building up a current in an inductor needs work against the back emf. That work is stored in the magnetic field: U = ½ L i². It is given back when the current falls.","f":[{"x":"U = (1/2)*L*i^2","n":"Energy stored in an inductor carrying current i.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 12.12, p.280"}]},"12.13":{"s":"The energy stored in a magnetic field per unit volume (energy density) is B²/(2μ₀). It depends only on the field, just as electric field energy depends on E.","f":[{"x":"u_B = B^2/(2*mu_0)","n":"Magnetic energy per unit volume.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 12.13, p.281"}]},"12.14":{"s":"When the current in one coil changes, it changes the flux through a nearby coil and induces an emf there: mutual induction. e₂ = −M di₁/dt, where M is the mutual inductance. M is larger when the coils are close, share an iron core, and have more turns.","f":[{"x":"e_2 = -M*dI_1/dt","n":"Emf in coil 2 from a changing current in coil 1; M_12 = M_21 = M.","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 12.14, p.282"},{"x":"M = K*sqrt(L_1*L_2)","n":"K = coefficient of coupling (at most 1; 1 for perfect coupling).","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 12.14, p.283"}],"w":["Coils with axes at right angles have the least coupling; on one iron core K is close to 1."]},"12.15":{"s":"A transformer changes AC voltage using mutual induction between two coils on an iron core. Vs/Vp = Ns/Np. A step-up transformer has more turns in the secondary; a step-down transformer has fewer. Ideally power in equals power out, so current changes the opposite way. It does not work with steady DC.","f":[{"x":"e_s/e_p = N_s/N_p = i_p/i_s","n":"Ideal transformer: voltage goes with turns, current the opposite way (power in = power out).","r":"Balbharati Physics XII, 12.15, p.285"}],"w":["Step-up raises voltage but lowers current; it does not make extra power."]}};
